---
title: Garlicky Baby Bok Choy (Syun Yeung Chau Siu Pak Choi 蔥油菜心)
description: Fast, vibrant garlicky greens to cut through rich meats. A weeknight side dish with crispy garlic and toasted sesame oil finish.
prep time: 5 minutes
cook time: 5 minutes
servings: 2
tags:
  - bok choy
  - vegetable
  - quick
  - chinese
  - side
source: https://thewoksoflife.com/bok-choy
cuisine: Chinese
course: Side
---

> Keep the heat high and the moves fast. Bok choy goes from crisp to mushy quickly, and you want bright green color with a slight bite in the stems. The steam-finish trick cooks the thicker parts without overcooking the leaves.

== Fry Aromatics ==

Heat your #wok{} over high heat until screaming hot. Add @neutral oil{1%tbsp} and swirl to coat.

Add the @crushed garlic{5%cloves} (not minced—crushed pieces are less likely to burn). Fry for ~{10%seconds} until just golden. Immediately add the @halved baby bok choy{600%g} cut-side down.

== Toss and Steam ==

Toss vigorously to coat the greens with the garlic oil. Add:
- @Shaoxing wine{1%tbsp}
- @salt{½%tsp}
- @sugar{¼%tsp}

Toss again. If the wok looks dry, add @water{1%tbsp} around the edges. Immediately cover with a #lid{} and steam for ~{30%seconds}—this finishes cooking the thicker stems without softening the leaves too much.

== Finish ==

Remove the lid (the steam will have made the garlic crispy rather than burned). Drizzle with @toasted sesame oil{1%tsp}, give one final toss, and plate.

Serve immediately—this dish does not hold well and will continue to cook in its own residual heat.